  Negative is a win. The sweep scored A=7,920 at -0.265 ms on yolov8s and +0.143 ms on yolov8n.
  The bar it was judged against was 0.29 ms (the same-sitting gap to AMD on yolov8s).

CONCLUSION
The sweep's verdict rests on a split it got wrong by construction: it charged MemTile-to-core bytes at
the DDR rate. Correct that and the activation path is task-bound far harder than it claimed - 80% of the
floor is per-task waiting, not 38%. Every candidate's score therefore moves in favour of LARGER packets,
because shrinking A multiplies the term that was under-counted and growing A reduces it.

A = 7,920 B - the sweep's own optimum, which it rejected for a +0.143 ms regression on YOLOv8n - scores
-0.51 to -0.81 ms on YOLOv8n and -1.40 to -2.04 ms on YOLOv8s once the task term is priced as measured.
Both are past the 0.29 ms bar the lever was judged against, and the sign flips on YOLOv8n.
A = 9,472 B is the core-L1 ceiling at ObjectFIFO depth 2 (65,536 - 59,392 = 6,144 B free, two buffers).

WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH
- The 1/A task scaling is crude. A faithful re-sweep must redo the per-kind geometry search
  (engine_schedule.CHUNK_GEOMETRY and a_pattern) for each candidate, which is what the original did and
  what no committed script can currently do.
- The bytes term is given as a range because the shim-side byte growth of a larger packet depends on that
  same geometry search: the low end assumes the MemTile's re-serving absorbs it, the high end assumes
  shim bytes grow proportionally with A.
- Nothing is measured at any A other than 6,400. Building one resized container and running this
  session's NOP tool on it would settle the direction in a single sitting.

WHAT IT WOULD COST TO TRY
Compiler: engine_emulator.A_BYTES and GEOM_*, engine_schedule.CHUNK_GEOMETRY and all seven a_pattern
shapes, Placement's junk-plane sizing, engine_compile's manifest "a_bytes". Overlay: an xclbin rebuild,
because kernels/aie2/conv_engine/design.py:30 bakes A_BYTES into a_ty and the ObjectFifo split. NOT
engine.cc - it reads rows_in/cols_in/plane_bytes from the packet header at runtime.
Also worth fixing first: A_BYTES is a bare literal duplicated in src/ and kernels/ with no gate keeping
them equal, unlike WBUF_BYTES which has both a comment and a source-reading check.